  "ai-comic": "# AI comic\n\nFor short comic-style stories and motion comics.\n\nWorkflow:\n1. Find the story, characters, art style (manga, western comic, webtoon, watercolor) and the length or panel count.\n2. One panel per shot. The art style goes in the option's `look` (\"flat-color manga, black ink outlines\") and every character in `characters`, so each panel uses the same words.\n3. Propose 2-3 options that differ in art style or framing.\n\nShot conventions:\n- Default 4-6 panels of 4-5 s each (about 20-24 s).\n- Gentle motion only in `action`: hair, cloth, light, a blink, a slow turn. Camera `static`, `push_in` or a slow pan. No fast action.\n- Speech bubbles or captions go in `line`, short, in the user's language.\n- Characters are adults unless the user clearly says otherwise; never sexualize anyone.\n\nModels: the default video model; a cheaper fast model for a draft option.\n",
  "brand-kit": "# Brand kit\n\nFor building a set of brand visuals: logo animation, product stills, patterns and short loops in one consistent look.\n\nWorkflow:\n1. Find the brand name, colors, type of product, tone and any `@` logo or product images.\n2. Plan a small set of short shots that share one look: an intro sting, a product hero, a texture or pattern loop, an end card.\n3. Propose 2-3 options that differ in visual direction (minimal, bold color, natural materials).\n\nShot conventions:\n- Default length 16 s: 4 shots of 4 s.\n- Name the brand colors in the option's `look` (for example \"deep green #0B3D2E and off-white, matte, minimal\"); it is applied to every shot.\n- A product or mascot that appears more than once goes in `characters` (kind `object` for a product).\n- End card: a plain background in the brand color, camera `static`. With an `@` logo the keyframe uses it as a reference; with none, leave the centre clear for the logo and do not invent a logo or a name.\n- No lines unless the user gives a tagline; then put it on the end card only.\n\nModels: the default video model; images use the default image model for keyframes.\n",
  "product-showcase": "# Product showcase\n\nFor product pages and social posts: show one product's look, details and use.\n\nWorkflow:\n1. Find the product, the features to show, the ratio and any `@` product photos.\n2. Order shots from wide to detail to in-use, then a clean end frame.\n3. Propose 2-3 options: for example studio turntable, lifestyle setting, macro details.\n\nShot conventions:\n- Default length 12 s, 3 shots of 4 s. 20 s: 4-5 shots.\n- The product goes in `characters` with kind `object` (look = shape, material, color, logo placement; no wardrobe) and in every shot's `cast`: material, color and logo placement stay identical across shots. Describe only what the brief or the product photo gives; do not invent a brand name or a logo.\n- Studio options: neutral or brand-colored background in `setting`, soft even light. Lifestyle options: a real setting.\n- Slow moves only: `orbit`, `push_in`, `truck_left` or `truck_right`, or `static` for macro details. No on-screen text in the picture.\n- Lines are optional: short callouts of features the brief names, never invented specifications.\n\nModels: the default video model; for 4-second macro shots a fast model is acceptable.\n",
  "storyboard": "# Storyboard\n\nFor turning a story, script or idea into shots before any video is made. Also for short dramas with recurring characters.\n\nWorkflow:\n1. Find the story beats, the characters, the setting and the target length.\n2. One shot per beat. Every character that appears more than once goes in `characters` with a fixed `look` and `wardrobe`, and in `cast` of each shot it is in. Locations keep the same `setting` words.\n3. Propose 2-3 options that differ in pacing or visual style (for example live action vs. animated look, set in `look`).\n\nShot conventions:\n- Default length 30 s, 6 shots of about 5 s.\n- Vary shot sizes: an establishing `wide`, then `medium`, `close_up`, an insert. Do not repeat the same size three times in a row.\n- A conversation: alternate `over_the_shoulder` or `medium_close_up` shots, one speaker per shot, eyelines facing each other.\n- Spoken words go in `dialogue` (a model with audio, one short line per shot) when the user wants characters to speak; narration goes in `line`. A shot has one or the other, not both. Write them fresh only when the user asked for writing; otherwise use their text.\n\nModels: the default video model. Keyframes matter most here; keep `subject` and `action` specific.\n",
